  'Classical Social Theory',
  'Classical Social Theory',
  'This course introduces the major questions, texts, and debates that shaped classical social theory. Students study how early sociologists and social theorists explained modernity, capitalism, bureaucracy, religion, urban life, race, gender, social order, conflict, power, and inequality. The course centers Marx, Durkheim, and Weber while also treating Comte, Simmel, Du Bois, Martineau, Gilman, and the Chicago School as essential to understanding the emergence and limits of sociology.',
  $clstjson${"slug": "classical-social-theory", "title": "Classical Social Theory", "description": "This course introduces the major questions, texts, and debates that shaped classical social theory. Students study how early sociologists and social theorists explained modernity, capitalism, bureaucracy, religion, urban life, race, gender, social order, conflict, power, and inequality. The course centers Marx, Durkheim, and Weber while also treating Comte, Simmel, Du Bois, Martineau, Gilman, and the Chicago School as essential to understanding the emergence and limits of sociology.", "topics": [{"id": "c481593f-1832-4a79-a219-672c4f160332", "type": "topic", "title": "Origins and Stakes of Classical Social Theory", "description": "This opening unit explains why classical social theory still matters. Students learn to read theory as a set of tools for naming social forces, not as museum pieces from the past.", "children": [{"id": "2415483b-be27-438d-b125-5daa5e844c02", "type": "subtopic", "title": "What Makes Theory Classical", "children": [{"id": "d1a77c7b-0559-453f-8af8-39c4ee74e22c", "type": "concept", "title": "Foundational questions and durable concepts"}, {"id": "9b8fb52b-4398-43cc-a531-7e6d238c222e", "type": "concept", "title": "Original texts versus later interpretation"}, {"id": "6a6cccc8-e323-41fe-8e73-00fc5f805a2f", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon formation and exclusion"}, {"id": "d8dcefaa-8965-489a-a894-6db4516151b3", "type": "concept", "title": "Classical theory as a living vocabulary"}], "description": "Students learn why certain texts become canonical and how canons are made, contested, expanded, and used."}, {"id": "703aa0c6-9eac-46be-b6a9-5f46c3cc285a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The Sociological Imagination", "children": [{"id": "1746d5cd-3c47-4b5c-abc7-807a52f07010", "type": "concept", "title": "Private troubles and public issues"}, {"id": "3313791c-5781-4d44-a24a-6da818b7e633", "type": "concept", "title": "Biography, history, and social structure"}, {"id": "10e2ad5b-1e4b-4c21-8cb0-753441bb8eff", "type": "concept", "title": "Common sense versus sociological explanation"}, {"id": "01539922-01c3-4cc3-8532-ec076908ef68", "type": "concept", "title": "Theory as disciplined distance from everyday assumptions"}], "description": "The point is to connect personal experience to historical and institutional conditions."}, {"id": "caedd222-0a04-4530-a8c4-d9612e77a90f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Order, Conflict, Meaning, and Change", "children": [{"id": "96cdeb12-a034-466d-a6b7-3596586da9bd", "type": "concept", "title": "How social order is possible"}, {"id": "edbc5a69-3f33-4e2f-8d5f-3f07dc1172ff", "type": "concept", "title": "Why inequality persists"}, {"id": "90f5a812-c3c5-4392-9005-5b67447bd2ae", "type": "concept", "title": "How actors create meaning"}, {"id": "e48df145-2784-4459-ac6b-4e9b1bbc6ef9", "type": "concept", "title": "How societies transform and break down"}], "description": "Students learn the major questions that organize the whole course."}]}, {"id": "5f9ef976-e147-405f-af74-da4f38e9d8a1", "type": "topic", "title": "Modernity, Revolution, Industrial Capitalism, and the Birth of Sociology", "description": "Classical theory emerged from upheaval. Students study the historical conditions that made society appear as something that could be analyzed, compared, governed, and changed.", "children": [{"id": "34d565a2-9814-4a01-8c98-ca975b07a3a7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Enlightenment and Revolution", "children": [{"id": "8ae546d4-fbca-4ee6-8420-a1b5ce61d02b", "type": "concept", "title": "Enlightenment reason and progress"}, {"id": "f1ff4456-9aac-48a0-b1fd-b33987620eab", "type": "concept", "title": "French and American revolutions"}, {"id": "675736cb-75ea-428a-a2cb-5731454ec4bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Citizenship and sovereignty"}, {"id": "c7813936-1c8d-4589-8d03-6389cd4123d4", "type": "concept", "title": "The problem of social order after tradition"}], "description": "Students learn how reason, rights, secular authority, and democratic revolution reshaped theories of society."}, {"id": "a8ab0013-c22c-45e6-a0d0-579ff0b5479a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Industrial Capitalism", "children": [{"id": "56d6fb42-a3c4-45d5-80ca-85c54ed49524", "type": "concept", "title": "Industrialization and labor discipline"}, {"id": "21dad20e-20cb-4db3-883d-983f2de6271c", "type": "concept", "title": "Capital accumulation and markets"}, {"id": "13e3ff70-733e-4e24-a630-cd2fae3bbb32", "type": "concept", "title": "Class formation"}, {"id": "7d6eeb41-e492-4fda-8f34-0c581ce7627a", "type": "concept", "title": "Poverty, exploitation, and reform"}], "description": "The gist is understanding how wage labor, factories, markets, and urban poverty forced new explanations of class and social change."}, {"id": "13ff82f7-9201-4187-a543-2414ddbb0f8d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Empire, Coloniality, and Global Modernity", "children": [{"id": "0bc0363f-1a8d-4d33-b63c-27902690dbe9", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial expansion"}, {"id": "7ff9440d-f8f8-46c6-924f-baf8f7d11dab", "type": "concept", "title": "Slavery and abolition"}, {"id": "8a8ba34a-2ec2-4eee-b19f-87dbc8f71ace", "type": "concept", "title": "Race science and empire"}, {"id": "725f1615-9561-4a0e-84b7-b0ddcc4ccf5c", "type": "concept", "title": "Global uneven development"}], "description": "Students learn that classical theory was produced inside a global order shaped by conquest, slavery, extraction, and racial hierarchy."}]}, {"id": "1e872f1c-3b05-4e1c-a868-7d8115283b5f", "type": "topic", "title": "Auguste Comte, Positivism, and the Scientific Ambition of Sociology", "description": "Comte gives sociology one of its earliest self-descriptions as a science of society. Students learn both the ambition and the danger of trying to model social life after natural science.", "children": [{"id": "b6dab524-4110-43d7-a103-600a7691564d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Positivism", "children": [{"id": "15b4d1c9-11b7-4933-a775-6b58db234940", "type": "concept", "title": "Observation and classification"}, {"id": "f777841a-0f66-4b42-81cb-9958fec55e9c", "type": "concept", "title": "Social laws and prediction"}, {"id": "fc189222-5524-414d-bbcd-62998cd1a79f", "type": "concept", "title": "Hierarchy of the sciences"}, {"id": "5dc7b30b-3fe7-42ee-979d-8cff77f9a564", "type": "concept", "title": "Limits of positivist certainty"}], "description": "Students learn the appeal of empirical, law-seeking social science and the critiques it invites."}, {"id": "91c3fde0-f3b4-4915-a474-94c2094f8305", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Social Statics and Social Dynamics", "children": [{"id": "054d39f5-38e7-4b98-8aeb-f1e78bfc324c", "type": "concept", "title": "Institutions and cohesion"}, {"id": "25f72339-9a8a-4847-bf50-0dd0419ea061", "type": "concept", "title": "Progress and development"}, {"id": "eaabd61c-d099-4bd0-94d6-a63155ddc69f", "type": "concept", "title": "Consensus and moral order"}, {"id": "8ddcca14-56b9-4d92-a4aa-c9319cf1ccf8", "type": "concept", "title": "Evolutionary assumptions"}], "description": "The point is to distinguish theories of order from theories of change."}, {"id": "6bcaa226-5ad6-4c69-9dcd-a7e725c016ce", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comte's Legacy and Limits", "children": [{"id": "a0fd96b4-46a2-4830-9185-831d8bc423e7", "type": "concept", "title": "Disciplinary naming and ambition"}, {"id": "e313e1ef-ac98-4f60-b6bf-ee608fb24b7e", "type": "concept", "title": "Science, reform, and technocracy"}, {"id": "50f6782d-c927-4dd6-9b98-dff386e42085", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender and hierarchy in Comte's thought"}, {"id": "e6b93c7f-be39-4f12-a1ef-27a2ad227c3f", "type": "concept", "title": "Positivism in later sociology"}], "description": "Students learn why Comte matters even when his system seems dated."}]}, {"id": "2c0a6d77-063a-486b-98b3-870058296941", "type": "topic", "title": "Karl Marx: Historical Materialism, Capitalism, Class, and Alienation", "description": "Marx offers a powerful theory of capitalism as a historical system organized by labor, exploitation, class struggle, and crisis. Students learn to connect economic relations to ideology, law, politics, and everyday life.", "children": [{"id": "2d9c8c28-34ac-499d-b06b-7e1ef72f18b7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historical Materialism", "children": [{"id": "46d78894-728e-45cd-ab4a-290daa624776", "type": "concept", "title": "Forces and relations of production"}, {"id": "f9b14b79-c73b-4ec5-be49-0211ff226ee1", "type": "concept", "title": "Base and superstructure debates"}, {"id": "ddf8ec52-80c4-42f1-9b03-670aced6ac06", "type": "concept", "title": "Modes of production"}, {"id": "e54ba0ae-1255-46ea-89ab-1e1832237b89", "type": "concept", "title": "Contradiction and historical change"}], "description": "Students learn Marx's method for explaining social change through production, class relations, and contradiction."}, {"id": "a482cd8b-864d-48ca-926f-36e6752a821c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Capitalism and Exploitation", "children": [{"id": "78b07b1f-88a0-4641-bae6-86c45cdd8c47", "type": "concept", "title": "Commodity and commodity fetishism"}, {"id": "a2eeee8b-0c5e-46a0-9c76-e4ce9a02fed4", "type": "concept", "title": "Surplus value and exploitation"}, {"id": "f1703427-2fa8-490e-a932-e90bef9e97d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Alienation and labor"}, {"id": "e3732704-5a50-4086-9aa1-b0004b86f1ac", "type": "concept", "title": "Accumulation, crisis, and competition"}], "description": "The gist is understanding capitalism as a social relation, not just a market system."}, {"id": "3c6d1876-a8b1-4d76-93bb-c4dadf2e8a75", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Class, Ideology, and Revolution", "children": [{"id": "2142ca17-deb4-4c19-b05c-42194ea4be5f", "type": "concept", "title": "Bourgeoisie and proletariat"}, {"id": "1c4beaec-8ed9-4cf5-a19a-966447aea294", "type": "concept", "title": "Class in itself and for itself"}, {"id": "b3c189c7-10b7-4bee-8249-4f6ac4ce5e8c", "type": "concept", "title": "Ideology and false consciousness"}, {"id": "23eb97d3-7946-4263-a6bd-a86b6118e757", "type": "concept", "title": "Revolution, reform, and critique"}], "description": "Students learn how Marx links material interests to political struggle and consciousness."}]}, {"id": "9546232f-1192-48c1-abfd-36d16cdfe291", "type": "topic", "title": "Emile Durkheim: Social Facts, Solidarity, Anomie, and Suicide", "description": "Durkheim asks how societies hold together and what happens when moral regulation breaks down. Students learn the logic of social facts, solidarity, anomie, suicide, and ritual.", "children": [{"id": "20859779-6c62-4b78-b924-77ff7535c9dd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Social Facts and Method", "children": [{"id": "a1e6315f-74c5-47c3-94be-24870de315ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Externality and constraint"}, {"id": "8d39db41-8720-4dea-bc36-e6b90d420a92", "type": "concept", "title": "Rules of sociological method"}, {"id": "bd8b3038-ba92-460d-9c8f-c486c57d111d", "type": "concept", "title": "Collective representations"}, {"id": "954e6094-aedf-4045-a31a-ea63c8c83585", "type": "concept", "title": "Normal and pathological forms"}], "description": "Students learn Durkheim's claim that social phenomena must be treated as realities external to individuals."}, {"id": "c9a5100d-9e97-4e09-b155-a7b8a2a11ba2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Solidarity and Division of Labor", "children": [{"id": "198fe5dc-1530-47d3-9346-55ac8ab86286", "type": "concept", "title": "Mechanical solidarity"}, {"id": "6be748b5-35e9-421f-aabd-bba13ed55e24", "type": "concept", "title": "Organic solidarity"}, {"id": "c8457cf3-3821-4b91-96a8-284b7c276dee", "type": "concept", "title": "Law as an index of solidarity"}, {"id": "1046807b-5322-4fee-a831-543951149c1e", "type": "concept", "title": "Anomie and forced division of labor"}], "description": "The point is to compare cohesion in traditional and modern societies."}, {"id": "5437299e-741f-4ccd-b47e-f2fa1fce5ab3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Suicide, Religion, and Ritual", "children": [{"id": "407d5b50-6dc6-45ab-95e3-fc70b82d94bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Egoistic, altruistic, anomic, and fatalistic suicide"}, {"id": "f138e63d-e377-4902-8435-0d4f7c2bd2c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Integration and regulation"}, {"id": "0f1ea616-55eb-437b-a9c7-230f54537c2f", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacred and profane"}, {"id": "cf5bb3ba-fd54-4032-96d2-862d8f1fc584", "type": "concept", "title": "Ritual, classification, and collective effervescence"}], "description": "Students learn how Durkheim connects individual action to social integration and collective meaning."}]}, {"id": "56abe1ed-beed-4529-8ace-13bedc556e84", "type": "topic", "title": "Max Weber: Interpretive Sociology, Rationalization, Authority, and Bureaucracy", "description": "Weber teaches students to analyze meaning, power, legitimacy, and rationalization. His work helps explain modern bureaucracy, authority, capitalism, religion, and the tension between values and facts.", "children": [{"id": "d5990417-2e12-4651-bc9a-622776c0e16d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Interpretive Sociology", "children": [{"id": "3fc6c34f-ae64-4066-b2bd-f86488b92809", "type": "concept", "title": "Verstehen"}, {"id": "4fef0ebe-b56c-41d8-85be-5d9bc08a1d42", "type": "concept", "title": "Types of social action"}, {"id": "f8ab74dc-9e3b-4c1a-83b6-fc63cbff2424", "type": "concept", "title": "Ideal types"}, {"id": "61e3cbcd-1756-45fe-976d-6b511dbda9fd", "type": "concept", "title": "Value relevance and objectivity"}], "description": "Students learn Weber's method for understanding meaningful social action without reducing it to psychology."}, {"id": "e51faaf0-4a93-436f-bbad-93f2e8b8ef2b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Rationalization and Disenchantment", "children": [{"id": "df4ca1ba-731d-4bf0-9c91-1ffa88941ac7", "type": "concept", "title": "Formal and substantive rationality"}, {"id": "6176b410-0b7b-4f51-a9e1-d22e56c96b03", "type": "concept", "title": "The Protestant ethic thesis"}, {"id": "16884b17-cfcd-4b55-9d86-ec83862074b8", "type": "concept", "title": "Disenchantment"}, {"id": "75120ea2-0687-4483-9c26-e6e8cc4a095a", "type": "concept", "title": "The iron cage"}], "description": "The gist is learning Weber's account of modern life as increasingly calculable, rule-bound, and disenchanted."}, {"id": "41d80c5b-aebc-47d8-8eba-f6bb95fc34c9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Authority, Bureaucracy, and Domination", "children": [{"id": "85ece242-3981-4c1c-bc36-2dd655d10396", "type": "concept", "title": "Traditional, charismatic, and legal-rational authority"}, {"id": "52a2b785-1ffc-41bc-8b4c-775b887f7b94", "type": "concept", "title": "Bureaucratic organization"}, {"id": "e74dc3bb-34f9-4ac1-a517-8e83b4b541e2", "type": "concept", "title": "Status groups and parties"}, {"id": "71ab5bcb-6a5b-4e01-894e-f19da18f8f36", "type": "concept", "title": "Politics, ethics, and responsibility"}], "description": "Students learn how power becomes legitimate and how modern administration shapes social life."}]}, {"id": "14beafe7-6380-4b69-9708-46377d4e4b58", "type": "topic", "title": "Georg Simmel: Forms of Association, Money, Metropolis, and Strangerhood", "description": "Simmel helps students see social life in forms of interaction: exchange, conflict, secrecy, sociability, distance, and individuality. His work bridges micro encounters and macro modernity.", "children": [{"id": "28c9b4e4-0e0a-400f-b651-ea0fae72566a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Forms of Association", "children": [{"id": "f82bc059-3a4a-4217-a2d6-3449853145e8", "type": "concept", "title": "Dyads and triads"}, {"id": "097d0332-54e4-498c-8bce-3be94dfa30ee", "type": "concept", "title": "Exchange and reciprocity"}, {"id": "d5951c1a-8bba-418b-a97b-ff3a86460189", "type": "concept", "title": "Conflict as social form"}, {"id": "e8db694f-a0b1-47f0-a321-83852b102f5a", "type": "concept", "title": "Sociability, secrecy, and trust"}], "description": "Students learn to analyze recurring interaction patterns across different social contents."}, {"id": "5c8e2c69-9301-4ff3-ae0b-a4910adc9293", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Money and Modern Life", "children": [{"id": "201b81f6-1d8b-4040-aecb-1a53cb21b345", "type": "concept", "title": "The philosophy of money"}, {"id": "74eb78e1-aaef-4ec1-ada2-aeec32275184", "type": "concept", "title": "Objective and subjective culture"}, {"id": "2a4708d5-508e-4e57-8869-f322bca46d5d", "type": "concept", "title": "Calculation and abstraction"}, {"id": "2cbb5cc4-bbef-41e9-bf2c-3ce0ffd228a1", "type": "concept", "title": "Freedom, dependence, and impersonality"}], "description": "The point is to understand how money transforms value, individuality, relationships, and social distance."}, {"id": "be4eb59c-5f30-4e14-9a33-096dd5d57f2b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The Metropolis and the Stranger", "children": [{"id": "001715ae-7d11-477e-a8f5-a9f1b6f460a5", "type": "concept", "title": "Blase attitude"}, {"id": "a1e47a78-6d60-47a5-913a-c3c051466de9", "type": "concept", "title": "Nervous stimulation"}, {"id": "6c120b82-0da2-4341-a450-3e1d40ad51eb", "type": "concept", "title": "The stranger as near and far"}, {"id": "c582f6d1-6e03-4166-8b25-d47fb46843b4", "type": "concept", "title": "Individuality and modern culture"}], "description": "Students learn how urban life produces distinctive forms of perception, identity, and social distance."}]}, {"id": "577f8c42-a1fa-48ae-8d06-546e1faefb58", "type": "topic", "title": "W.E.B. Du Bois: The Color Line, Double Consciousness, and Racialized Modernity", "description": "Du Bois belongs at the center of classical theory. Students learn how race, empire, capitalism, knowledge, and democracy are inseparable in his account of modern society.", "children": [{"id": "5453d26d-8fc7-4294-ab89-9c88795dc47f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The Color Line and Racialized Modernity", "children": [{"id": "92c9f37e-4e6d-4229-b2c1-b785b209a620", "type": "concept", "title": "The problem of the color line"}, {"id": "22e3278e-d585-43c0-9bea-9c86229dae8a", "type": "concept", "title": "Race, labor, and capitalism"}, {"id": "f856d725-5b6c-451f-8a36-c295ffeae2c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Empire and colonial domination"}, {"id": "985aa7bf-1f47-41e9-823f-2a305c06bf63", "type": "concept", "title": "Reconstruction and democracy"}], "description": "Students learn Du Bois's argument that the modern world is structured by global racial hierarchy."}, {"id": "401e6d8e-e588-414a-ba12-6f51f193dc19", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Double Consciousness and the Veil", "children": [{"id": "41083178-06c5-4055-871e-309461931b3b", "type": "concept", "title": "The veil"}, {"id": "e4508bc8-3341-4c10-84ba-40253a65f8b9", "type": "concept", "title": "Two-ness and self-consciousness"}, {"id": "4f895e1a-3d56-47e1-a2c9-ef234e4e7e1a", "type": "concept", "title": "Recognition and misrecognition"}, {"id": "40f2e118-9e69-44f1-9bd3-e5701b11e89f", "type": "concept", "title": "Identity, domination, and agency"}], "description": "The gist is understanding subjectivity under racial domination as a social and historical condition."}, {"id": "58dcbb52-a9ee-46f0-9db1-8e8cac19832a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Empirical Sociology and Public Scholarship", "children": [{"id": "5d9333a7-aee7-4087-885d-40b4d71b558f", "type": "concept", "title": "The Philadelphia Negro"}, {"id": "8ad01074-0fb3-4585-a405-f493c0db5d99", "type": "concept", "title": "Atlanta School sociology"}, {"id": "ada6f659-514e-4be6-874b-f649a6dcf6f3", "type": "concept", "title": "Data visualization and community study"}, {"id": "7dc342db-4b13-47ca-b304-e5af1e8ca496", "type": "concept", "title": "Scholarship, activism, and public sociology"}], "description": "Students learn Du Bois as a rigorous empirical researcher and public intellectual."}]}, {"id": "2f12cf7d-bc32-4e9f-b89b-8b3d0365f127", "type": "topic", "title": "Harriet Martineau and Charlotte Perkins Gilman: Gender, Political Economy, and Reform", "description": "This unit widens the classical canon. Martineau and Gilman show that early sociology included rigorous method, translation, gender analysis, political economy, and reformist critique.", "children": [{"id": "f2916a8e-da64-48c6-8aad-f52c50ab00c9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Harriet Martineau and Observational Method", "children": [{"id": "8888818d-6abd-422e-ab41-91c567147a42", "type": "concept", "title": "Society in America"}, {"id": "6ea74586-4d18-4d25-8ec7-e70f4263bb87", "type": "concept", "title": "Morals and manners"}, {"id": "f0f2ead4-f15f-4102-9b48-662b2407ffcc", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparative observation"}, {"id": "d706d630-ccea-4fd6-8ff2-02e618b6c916", "type": "concept", "title": "Abolition, democracy, and reform"}], "description": "Students learn Martineau's contributions to comparative observation, morals and manners, democracy, and social reform."}, {"id": "38c6f129-cafe-4ad3-bdd8-189b60d99624", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Charlotte Perkins Gilman and Gendered Political Economy", "children": [{"id": "6c7cbb81-5acb-4b57-864d-c15e0c3e7545", "type": "concept", "title": "Women and Economics"}, {"id": "e142d002-e247-4b9c-921d-9d98c3f1e53c", "type": "concept", "title": "Domestic labor and dependency"}, {"id": "47740a80-c987-4e17-91d2-9c8a14207057", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender socialization"}, {"id": "eab1a533-640f-41a6-be80-eab7919ddb6a", "type": "concept", "title": "Reform, utopia, and institutional change"}], "description": "The point is to analyze family, labor, dependency, and gender as social institutions rather than private arrangements."}, {"id": "abec580b-7d38-4e44-8443-bd1b98c0ac68", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Feminist Recovery of Classical Theory", "children": [{"id": "d6c490c3-c10c-4599-ae08-7e3c03c0b2ed", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon critique"}, {"id": "a3dd6ff3-d422-41d5-baf7-9cef7b48f68a", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender and knowledge production"}, {"id": "9b617f87-5baf-45ce-b958-0b9fcbfef2fe", "type": "concept", "title": "Public writing and reform"}, {"id": "536dc248-17c6-4f61-90aa-5829bb33eb72", "type": "concept", "title": "Intersection with race, class, and empire"}], "description": "Students learn how recovering excluded thinkers changes the questions theory asks."}]}, {"id": "7b025a23-8976-4f03-9e57-27e3232789d0", "type": "topic", "title": "Religion, Morality, Secularization, and Collective Life", "description": "Religion is central to classical theory because it reveals how societies create meaning, authority, discipline, morality, solidarity, and conflict.", "children": [{"id": "8deec7af-7bfd-4019-9098-bf3fbf7d467d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Durkheim on Ritual and Collective Life", "children": [{"id": "628a9995-3b48-4281-b4c7-6dcc80b91e26", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacred and profane"}, {"id": "9c399834-ca41-4d09-acd5-fee9ea1ff781", "type": "concept", "title": "Totemism and classification"}, {"id": "74f5615c-9c8f-463e-a11d-05368b93413b", "type": "concept", "title": "Collective effervescence"}, {"id": "704207c9-ec78-4cbe-801a-fa668f87ee63", "type": "concept", "title": "Ritual and solidarity"}], "description": "Students learn religion as a social fact that produces moral community."}, {"id": "32df2fbc-e645-4cfb-80d2-a95b03716908", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Weber on Religion and Capitalism", "children": [{"id": "27c1b573-93d5-412d-a5aa-08e49e011637", "type": "concept", "title": "Ascetic Protestantism"}, {"id": "d50370c4-0fb1-4e5e-8bf9-a39ca1582eb6", "type": "concept", "title": "Calling and discipline"}, {"id": "53eef9a3-e190-4293-9d99-dcf4d5d78c16", "type": "concept", "title": "World religions and comparative sociology"}, {"id": "dc6ba601-5754-43da-aa0e-dc0e5e71bfbe", "type": "concept", "title": "Disenchantment and secularization"}], "description": "The gist is understanding religion as an active force in economic and social organization."}, {"id": "883577f6-68c7-49cf-bb33-1a6652112e2b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Marx and Critical Accounts of Religion", "children": [{"id": "561d62c2-4559-4120-9045-1b0add91b1f1", "type": "concept", "title": "Religion and alienation"}, {"id": "cbcaf08b-ebc9-48eb-80bd-c3938254f65c", "type": "concept", "title": "Ideology and material conditions"}, {"id": "e91d0454-eca9-46fb-8fcf-59dbdb2e143f", "type": "concept", "title": "Legitimation and critique"}, {"id": "bff36f1b-9a04-43c3-83cd-ba78d86014eb", "type": "concept", "title": "Secularization debates"}], "description": "Students learn religion as consolation, ideology, protest, and social expression."}]}, {"id": "7d02ffee-8cc3-4e36-b46b-1dd70bd66711", "type": "topic", "title": "Urbanization, Immigration, the Chicago School, and Early Empirical Sociology", "description": "Early empirical sociology turned theory toward cities, migration, neighborhoods, race relations, and everyday social worlds. Students connect classical concepts to field research.", "children": [{"id": "af254c93-dad2-4806-baa7-b2101086b815", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Urbanization and Social Disorganization", "children": [{"id": "a5d366fb-d734-4572-9477-f713b604d024", "type": "concept", "title": "Industrial city and migration"}, {"id": "e54bf39b-02bf-4161-bf61-b05dca34e239", "type": "concept", "title": "Neighborhood ecology"}, {"id": "85b5d770-3ebe-4d66-84a9-1275d447a7d0", "type": "concept", "title": "Social disorganization"}, {"id": "ce74236c-e1ba-4e89-a4a4-74cde32742b6", "type": "concept", "title": "Community, poverty, and institutions"}], "description": "Students learn how rapid urban growth produced new questions about community, deviance, inequality, and social order."}, {"id": "af238291-6474-43f5-91a8-4ff905cb4a1b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The Chicago School", "children": [{"id": "153c8de1-26ce-4aa3-a595-127172fcc5bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Robert Park and Ernest Burgess"}, {"id": "fa5e7e85-aff6-4e69-a79b-bc9373c8a171", "type": "concept", "title": "Urban ecology"}, {"id": "5f49853e-3409-40f6-8743-6ddadd149b29", "type": "concept", "title": "Immigration and assimilation"}, {"id": "b07f6e67-84ed-4fa8-8496-5d874bc14156", "type": "concept", "title": "Case studies and ethnography"}], "description": "The point is to see how fieldwork, mapping, and urban observation became central to sociology."}, {"id": "328c29a8-eb43-4256-a25c-c34466aa2a4e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Race, Migration, and Urban Modernity", "children": [{"id": "d85786bc-63ae-4e6c-b651-c3d1d0a3fe63", "type": "concept", "title": "The ghetto and segregation"}, {"id": "16742ad3-82cb-486c-99ae-8df8516dcdbe", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethnic succession"}, {"id": "2ae51ba3-d6f6-454b-a1fd-8dc65eddda98", "type": "concept", "title": "Policing and deviance"}, {"id": "4034a98a-6c2c-4c51-8b59-89111ba23a04", "type": "concept", "title": "Du Bois and urban empirical sociology"}], "description": "Students learn to connect urban sociology to race, labor, segregation, and political power."}]}, {"id": "3890cb1b-5673-440d-bdb4-e0c437dbc46e", "type": "topic", "title": "Method, Evidence, Ideal Types, and Comparative-Historical Analysis", "description": "Theory is also method. Students learn how classical theorists constructed concepts, selected evidence, compared cases, and made arguments about history and society.", "children": [{"id": "880e56b2-65bc-4365-806e-d2b368f059e8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Close Reading and Conceptual Reconstruction", "children": [{"id": "b503d080-a707-4eb2-9e97-e25327040250", "type": "concept", "title": "Thesis, concept, and evidence"}, {"id": "b834efb6-7b0e-45a7-90c3-ae7b0b1d6c3e", "type": "concept", "title": "Contextual reading"}, {"id": "6831bb8f-edd6-41ba-8894-97a22a833660", "type": "concept", "title": "Key terms and definitions"}, {"id": "3daf7f26-eee6-4c6a-bda7-37883be201f5", "type": "concept", "title": "Interpretive charity and critique"}], "description": "Students learn to read difficult texts by identifying concepts, assumptions, claims, and tensions."}, {"id": "83874517-813a-4ae2-b012-97c54e566498", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ideal Types and Comparative-Historical Method", "children": [{"id": "488e1090-14f2-49d2-be84-04e4d679ec48", "type": "concept", "title": "Weberian ideal types"}, {"id": "a3d949ca-e878-4d04-9526-4d4edd84ecd1", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparative cases"}, {"id": "f2d3a02d-c7a5-480f-bacf-4ce7b0713f12", "type": "concept", "title": "Historical sequences"}, {"id": "eb922531-1d5b-460d-944b-340acde68db4", "type": "concept", "title": "Causal adequacy and interpretive adequacy"}], "description": "The gist is learning how theory turns messy reality into analyzable models without confusing models for reality."}, {"id": "4289c7ca-6d5f-403a-8560-70dd61688d18", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Evidence, Argument, and Writing Theory", "children": [{"id": "cd84b733-ebcf-4ab4-8382-1a8d252f7764", "type": "concept", "title": "Concept maps"}, {"id": "bee25c86-b334-4c03-ab37-534bf5724113", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparative matrices"}, {"id": "7cf383ab-f405-442a-8595-eae80e42f46a", "type": "concept", "title": "Textual evidence"}, {"id": "30b145ba-8ac0-4d9c-8247-0c72ff65b32a", "type": "concept", "title": "Application to empirical cases"}], "description": "Students learn to write theory papers that explain and evaluate concepts rather than merely summarize authors."}]}, {"id": "db49d705-4141-4267-86ce-45d71ea13c7f", "type": "topic", "title": "Power and Inequality: Class, Status, Race, Gender, Empire, and Coloniality", "description": "Classical theory is indispensable for studying inequality, but it must be read critically. Students learn how class, status, race, gender, empire, and coloniality shape modern power.", "children": [{"id": "4b3ce1b4-b43f-4334-afcc-247fe4ec217d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Class, Status, and Power", "children": [{"id": "0d513a43-9954-4eac-a34b-dc89b076b1c9", "type": "concept", "title": "Class relations"}, {"id": "e00115ac-26dc-4578-9062-c31a02dc07c4", "type": "concept", "title": "Status honor"}, {"id": "5219cac8-c5d6-4e92-9606-7aa690c627ae", "type": "concept", "title": "Party and power"}, {"id": "1bf9ea00-cd8e-40af-be04-aa3828a471e4", "type": "concept", "title": "Elites and domination"}], "description": "Students compare Marx's class analysis with Weber's multidimensional account of stratification."}, {"id": "254d70aa-0000-44fc-b12d-357852009f11", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Race, Gender, and Social Reproduction", "children": [{"id": "5f5ef73e-9d90-40be-bfb7-fe24f6fb3029", "type": "concept", "title": "Du Bois and racial capitalism"}, {"id": "6ee5034c-f1cd-4006-8658-9f8cc42b42b4", "type": "concept", "title": "Gilman and gendered labor"}, {"id": "e1e72249-f906-4a72-9d76-38220880ae5e", "type": "concept", "title": "Martineau and moral critique"}, {"id": "19d2b390-8ce6-46a3-ae5f-cd2d931ada6e", "type": "concept", "title": "Intersectional extensions"}], "description": "The point is to examine exclusions and recover theorists who analyzed domination beyond class alone."}, {"id": "2232de97-021a-48d7-85a0-00842fa3b0b9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Empire and Coloniality", "children": [{"id": "2ee2f605-e2f3-4426-bb75-563d28743ef5", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial labor and extraction"}, {"id": "68482d75-d5a3-4f5f-b93d-0679b357f8e2", "type": "concept", "title": "Race science and social hierarchy"}, {"id": "8581aabe-1008-4afc-b76f-3f75ab19f974", "type": "concept", "title": "Global color line"}, {"id": "87d6a758-506f-4425-b670-4904ab92b33f", "type": "concept", "title": "Decolonial critique of the canon"}], "description": "Students learn to ask how European modernity, capitalism, and theory were entangled with empire."}]}, {"id": "55a58ce3-ef79-4942-8895-0a4d3a61f1bd", "type": "topic", "title": "Comparing Classical Perspectives: Structure, Agency, Conflict, Order, and Meaning", "description": "This comparative unit helps students synthesize the course. They learn to use theory as a set of contrasting assumptions about human action, institutions, power, and social change.", "children": [{"id": "470a3222-a6f9-46b6-abc4-9cea51ce0374", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Structure and Agency", "children": [{"id": "2b1f1a89-192b-440d-9a5d-0371e1e4d048", "type": "concept", "title": "Marx and structural contradiction"}, {"id": "4247b937-5019-4794-a4a2-9373b1683f52", "type": "concept", "title": "Durkheim and social facts"}, {"id": "eefe0a74-488a-4220-b2bf-10194ca3b05b", "type": "concept", "title": "Weber and meaningful action"}, {"id": "5c74e1ff-3f8e-42d8-9bec-01f62848e74e", "type": "concept", "title": "Simmel and interaction forms"}], "description": "Students compare how theorists explain the relation between individual action and social constraint."}, {"id": "b27efa61-e748-4a36-8ecf-207806d43f8b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Conflict and Order", "children": [{"id": "9f9d5040-d3c2-4507-936e-0ab605d981be", "type": "concept", "title": "Class conflict"}, {"id": "bbe91f14-ed92-4107-a17b-1155a800f21c", "type": "concept", "title": "Moral integration"}, {"id": "be50cba5-8fcd-4fe6-bf51-cd84cd4fc679", "type": "concept", "title": "Legitimate domination"}, {"id": "1bde20cd-2b6e-44ce-95ef-e75afd4d4fb5", "type": "concept", "title": "Interactional tension and exchange"}], "description": "The gist is to compare theories that emphasize domination, cohesion, meaning, or institutional coordination."}, {"id": "f53f9dcf-4ee9-4e94-ad9f-0f31860ee76f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Meaning and Material Conditions", "children": [{"id": "e76718e5-2981-4e9d-8af8-ae8e3980bd08", "type": "concept", "title": "Ideology and material life"}, {"id": "c0425c38-e7e8-4ca4-b2ae-fb794b3836c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Religion and economic action"}, {"id": "0907f6ed-c84d-4c06-ad11-119561c1245b", "type": "concept", "title": "Collective representations"}, {"id": "3086d13c-bb33-4e6e-9653-b0e1e7ff5503", "type": "concept", "title": "Culture, money, race, and identity"}], "description": "Students learn to avoid false choices between culture and economy."}]}, {"id": "8eb51db3-8a2d-424a-84f8-27bc5294e819", "type": "topic", "title": "Classical Theory Today: Contemporary Relevance, Canon Critique, and Public Sociology", "description": "The final unit asks students to use classical theory now. Students apply concepts to contemporary problems while assessing what classical theory illuminates, distorts, or leaves out.", "children": [{"id": "e8c9f2d6-d7c5-4f4d-9135-2eabbf5aa17f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Applying Classical Theory to Contemporary Problems", "children": [{"id": "ea80ccbe-00c2-43dd-a3a6-794974f02734", "type": "concept", "title": "Platform capitalism and alienation"}, {"id": "40ec5605-d88d-4524-9e88-22713ba3aad7", "type": "concept", "title": "Bureaucracy, algorithms, and rationalization"}, {"id": "ea783c04-9eee-45f7-9eef-eb588dccc632", "type": "concept", "title": "Anomie, polarization, and social isolation"}, {"id": "77521bbb-d63b-457e-a403-deafb6451e27", "type": "concept", "title": "Race, inequality, and democracy"}], "description": "Students learn to use classical concepts in current analysis without forcing old frameworks onto new realities."}, {"id": "83ff1d00-fb69-4b8b-b7e3-8c6e0344757e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Critiquing the Canon", "children": [{"id": "ab2b9fd0-ee2c-4ced-bcf8-634ae9e41b22", "type": "concept", "title": "Eurocentrism and colonial blind spots"}, {"id": "c9f7b9a0-83e4-4360-84e0-ef41a64c9f45", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender exclusions"}, {"id": "5928ad04-424e-4bde-9a8d-dacbc693c31c", "type": "concept", "title": "Race and the marginalization of Du Bois"}, {"id": "d96e2e2f-5102-4df0-9b55-767f59f60633", "type": "concept", "title": "Translation, access, and disciplinary power"}], "description": "The point is to identify both enduring insight and historical limitation."}, {"id": "70a079b8-78a4-43fe-96e6-cb495f2642a9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Public Sociology and Theory as Practice", "children": [{"id": "604044eb-efdb-46cc-904f-7d0f1780b00d", "type": "concept", "title": "Theory-informed public writing"}, {"id": "003a6ca8-ddd2-462c-b092-70606003a225", "type": "concept", "title": "Concepts in policy debate"}, {"id": "c76d55d8-161a-40e5-8123-abbbb9b40ff7", "type": "concept", "title": "Critical reading of social claims"}, {"id": "7d286479-93e4-45b0-9f7a-265796e5d6ee", "type": "concept", "title": "Final synthesis and reflective practice"}], "description": "Students learn that theory can inform research, policy, activism, journalism, and civic reasoning."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$clstjson$::jsonb,
  true,
  false,
  'course',
  'public'
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.learning_paths where slug = 'classical-social-theory'
);

update public.learning_paths
set
  title = 'Classical Social Theory',
  goal = 'Classical Social Theory',
  summary = 'This course introduces the major questions, texts, and debates that shaped classical social theory. Students study how early sociologists and social theorists explained modernity, capitalism, bureaucracy, religion, urban life, race, gender, social order, conflict, power, and inequality. The course centers Marx, Durkheim, and Weber while also treating Comte, Simmel, Du Bois, Martineau, Gilman, and the Chicago School as essential to understanding the emergence and limits of sociology.',
  data = $clstjson${"slug": "classical-social-theory", "title": "Classical Social Theory", "description": "This course introduces the major questions, texts, and debates that shaped classical social theory. Students study how early sociologists and social theorists explained modernity, capitalism, bureaucracy, religion, urban life, race, gender, social order, conflict, power, and inequality. The course centers Marx, Durkheim, and Weber while also treating Comte, Simmel, Du Bois, Martineau, Gilman, and the Chicago School as essential to understanding the emergence and limits of sociology.", "topics": [{"id": "c481593f-1832-4a79-a219-672c4f160332", "type": "topic", "title": "Origins and Stakes of Classical Social Theory", "description": "This opening unit explains why classical social theory still matters. Students learn to read theory as a set of tools for naming social forces, not as museum pieces from the past.", "children": [{"id": "2415483b-be27-438d-b125-5daa5e844c02", "type": "subtopic", "title": "What Makes Theory Classical", "children": [{"id": "d1a77c7b-0559-453f-8af8-39c4ee74e22c", "type": "concept", "title": "Foundational questions and durable concepts"}, {"id": "9b8fb52b-4398-43cc-a531-7e6d238c222e", "type": "concept", "title": "Original texts versus later interpretation"}, {"id": "6a6cccc8-e323-41fe-8e73-00fc5f805a2f", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon formation and exclusion"}, {"id": "d8dcefaa-8965-489a-a894-6db4516151b3", "type": "concept", "title": "Classical theory as a living vocabulary"}], "description": "Students learn why certain texts become canonical and how canons are made, contested, expanded, and used."}, {"id": "703aa0c6-9eac-46be-b6a9-5f46c3cc285a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The Sociological Imagination", "children": [{"id": "1746d5cd-3c47-4b5c-abc7-807a52f07010", "type": "concept", "title": "Private troubles and public issues"}, {"id": "3313791c-5781-4d44-a24a-6da818b7e633", "type": "concept", "title": "Biography, history, and social structure"}, {"id": "10e2ad5b-1e4b-4c21-8cb0-753441bb8eff", "type": "concept", "title": "Common sense versus sociological explanation"}, {"id": "01539922-01c3-4cc3-8532-ec076908ef68", "type": "concept", "title": "Theory as disciplined distance from everyday assumptions"}], "description": "The point is to connect personal experience to historical and institutional conditions."}, {"id": "caedd222-0a04-4530-a8c4-d9612e77a90f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Order, Conflict, Meaning, and Change", "children": [{"id": "96cdeb12-a034-466d-a6b7-3596586da9bd", "type": "concept", "title": "How social order is possible"}, {"id": "edbc5a69-3f33-4e2f-8d5f-3f07dc1172ff", "type": "concept", "title": "Why inequality persists"}, {"id": "90f5a812-c3c5-4392-9005-5b67447bd2ae", "type": "concept", "title": "How actors create meaning"}, {"id": "e48df145-2784-4459-ac6b-4e9b1bbc6ef9", "type": "concept", "title": "How societies transform and break down"}], "description": "Students learn the major questions that organize the whole course."}]}, {"id": "5f9ef976-e147-405f-af74-da4f38e9d8a1", "type": "topic", "title": "Modernity, Revolution, Industrial Capitalism, and the Birth of Sociology", "description": "Classical theory emerged from upheaval. Students study the historical conditions that made society appear as something that could be analyzed, compared, governed, and changed.", "children": [{"id": "34d565a2-9814-4a01-8c98-ca975b07a3a7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Enlightenment and Revolution", "children": [{"id": "8ae546d4-fbca-4ee6-8420-a1b5ce61d02b", "type": "concept", "title": "Enlightenment reason and progress"}, {"id": "f1ff4456-9aac-48a0-b1fd-b33987620eab", "type": "concept", "title": "French and American revolutions"}, {"id": "675736cb-75ea-428a-a2cb-5731454ec4bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Citizenship and sovereignty"}, {"id": "c7813936-1c8d-4589-8d03-6389cd4123d4", "type": "concept", "title": "The problem of social order after tradition"}], "description": "Students learn how reason, rights, secular authority, and democratic revolution reshaped theories of society."}, {"id": "a8ab0013-c22c-45e6-a0d0-579ff0b5479a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Industrial Capitalism", "children": [{"id": "56d6fb42-a3c4-45d5-80ca-85c54ed49524", "type": "concept", "title": "Industrialization and labor discipline"}, {"id": "21dad20e-20cb-4db3-883d-983f2de6271c", "type": "concept", "title": "Capital accumulation and markets"}, {"id": "13e3ff70-733e-4e24-a630-cd2fae3bbb32", "type": "concept", "title": "Class formation"}, {"id": "7d6eeb41-e492-4fda-8f34-0c581ce7627a", "type": "concept", "title": "Poverty, exploitation, and reform"}], "description": "The gist is understanding how wage labor, factories, markets, and urban poverty forced new explanations of class and social change."}, {"id": "13ff82f7-9201-4187-a543-2414ddbb0f8d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Empire, Coloniality, and Global Modernity", "children": [{"id": "0bc0363f-1a8d-4d33-b63c-27902690dbe9", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial expansion"}, {"id": "7ff9440d-f8f8-46c6-924f-baf8f7d11dab", "type": "concept", "title": "Slavery and abolition"}, {"id": "8a8ba34a-2ec2-4eee-b19f-87dbc8f71ace", "type": "concept", "title": "Race science and empire"}, {"id": "725f1615-9561-4a0e-84b7-b0ddcc4ccf5c", "type": "concept", "title": "Global uneven development"}], "description": "Students learn that classical theory was produced inside a global order shaped by conquest, slavery, extraction, and racial hierarchy."}]}, {"id": "1e872f1c-3b05-4e1c-a868-7d8115283b5f", "type": "topic", "title": "Auguste Comte, Positivism, and the Scientific Ambition of Sociology", "description": "Comte gives sociology one of its earliest self-descriptions as a science of society. Students learn both the ambition and the danger of trying to model social life after natural science.", "children": [{"id": "b6dab524-4110-43d7-a103-600a7691564d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Positivism", "children": [{"id": "15b4d1c9-11b7-4933-a775-6b58db234940", "type": "concept", "title": "Observation and classification"}, {"id": "f777841a-0f66-4b42-81cb-9958fec55e9c", "type": "concept", "title": "Social laws and prediction"}, {"id": "fc189222-5524-414d-bbcd-62998cd1a79f", "type": "concept", "title": "Hierarchy of the sciences"}, {"id": "5dc7b30b-3fe7-42ee-979d-8cff77f9a564", "type": "concept", "title": "Limits of positivist certainty"}], "description": "Students learn the appeal of empirical, law-seeking social science and the critiques it invites."}, {"id": "91c3fde0-f3b4-4915-a474-94c2094f8305", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Social Statics and Social Dynamics", "children": [{"id": "054d39f5-38e7-4b98-8aeb-f1e78bfc324c", "type": "concept", "title": "Institutions and cohesion"}, {"id": "25f72339-9a8a-4847-bf50-0dd0419ea061", "type": "concept", "title": "Progress and development"}, {"id": "eaabd61c-d099-4bd0-94d6-a63155ddc69f", "type": "concept", "title": "Consensus and moral order"}, {"id": "8ddcca14-56b9-4d92-a4aa-c9319cf1ccf8", "type": "concept", "title": "Evolutionary assumptions"}], "description": "The point is to distinguish theories of order from theories of change."}, {"id": "6bcaa226-5ad6-4c69-9dcd-a7e725c016ce", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comte's Legacy and Limits", "children": [{"id": "a0fd96b4-46a2-4830-9185-831d8bc423e7", "type": "concept", "title": "Disciplinary naming and ambition"}, {"id": "e313e1ef-ac98-4f60-b6bf-ee608fb24b7e", "type": "concept", "title": "Science, reform, and technocracy"}, {"id": "50f6782d-c927-4dd6-9b98-dff386e42085", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender and hierarchy in Comte's thought"}, {"id": "e6b93c7f-be39-4f12-a1ef-27a2ad227c3f", "type": "concept", "title": "Positivism in later sociology"}], "description": "Students learn why Comte matters even when his system seems dated."}]}, {"id": "2c0a6d77-063a-486b-98b3-870058296941", "type": "topic", "title": "Karl Marx: Historical Materialism, Capitalism, Class, and Alienation", "description": "Marx offers a powerful theory of capitalism as a historical system organized by labor, exploitation, class struggle, and crisis. Students learn to connect economic relations to ideology, law, politics, and everyday life.", "children": [{"id": "2d9c8c28-34ac-499d-b06b-7e1ef72f18b7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historical Materialism", "children": [{"id": "46d78894-728e-45cd-ab4a-290daa624776", "type": "concept", "title": "Forces and relations of production"}, {"id": "f9b14b79-c73b-4ec5-be49-0211ff226ee1", "type": "concept", "title": "Base and superstructure debates"}, {"id": "ddf8ec52-80c4-42f1-9b03-670aced6ac06", "type": "concept", "title": "Modes of production"}, {"id": "e54ba0ae-1255-46ea-89ab-1e1832237b89", "type": "concept", "title": "Contradiction and historical change"}], "description": "Students learn Marx's method for explaining social change through production, class relations, and contradiction."}, {"id": "a482cd8b-864d-48ca-926f-36e6752a821c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Capitalism and Exploitation", "children": [{"id": "78b07b1f-88a0-4641-bae6-86c45cdd8c47", "type": "concept", "title": "Commodity and commodity fetishism"}, {"id": "a2eeee8b-0c5e-46a0-9c76-e4ce9a02fed4", "type": "concept", "title": "Surplus value and exploitation"}, {"id": "f1703427-2fa8-490e-a932-e90bef9e97d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Alienation and labor"}, {"id": "e3732704-5a50-4086-9aa1-b0004b86f1ac", "type": "concept", "title": "Accumulation, crisis, and competition"}], "description": "The gist is understanding capitalism as a social relation, not just a market system."}, {"id": "3c6d1876-a8b1-4d76-93bb-c4dadf2e8a75", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Class, Ideology, and Revolution", "children": [{"id": "2142ca17-deb4-4c19-b05c-42194ea4be5f", "type": "concept", "title": "Bourgeoisie and proletariat"}, {"id": "1c4beaec-8ed9-4cf5-a19a-966447aea294", "type": "concept", "title": "Class in itself and for itself"}, {"id": "b3c189c7-10b7-4bee-8249-4f6ac4ce5e8c", "type": "concept", "title": "Ideology and false consciousness"}, {"id": "23eb97d3-7946-4263-a6bd-a86b6118e757", "type": "concept", "title": "Revolution, reform, and critique"}], "description": "Students learn how Marx links material interests to political struggle and consciousness."}]}, {"id": "9546232f-1192-48c1-abfd-36d16cdfe291", "type": "topic", "title": "Emile Durkheim: Social Facts, Solidarity, Anomie, and Suicide", "description": "Durkheim asks how societies hold together and what happens when moral regulation breaks down. Students learn the logic of social facts, solidarity, anomie, suicide, and ritual.", "children": [{"id": "20859779-6c62-4b78-b924-77ff7535c9dd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Social Facts and Method", "children": [{"id": "a1e6315f-74c5-47c3-94be-24870de315ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Externality and constraint"}, {"id": "8d39db41-8720-4dea-bc36-e6b90d420a92", "type": "concept", "title": "Rules of sociological method"}, {"id": "bd8b3038-ba92-460d-9c8f-c486c57d111d", "type": "concept", "title": "Collective representations"}, {"id": "954e6094-aedf-4045-a31a-ea63c8c83585", "type": "concept", "title": "Normal and pathological forms"}], "description": "Students learn Durkheim's claim that social phenomena must be treated as realities external to individuals."}, {"id": "c9a5100d-9e97-4e09-b155-a7b8a2a11ba2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Solidarity and Division of Labor", "children": [{"id": "198fe5dc-1530-47d3-9346-55ac8ab86286", "type": "concept", "title": "Mechanical solidarity"}, {"id": "6be748b5-35e9-421f-aabd-bba13ed55e24", "type": "concept", "title": "Organic solidarity"}, {"id": "c8457cf3-3821-4b91-96a8-284b7c276dee", "type": "concept", "title": "Law as an index of solidarity"}, {"id": "1046807b-5322-4fee-a831-543951149c1e", "type": "concept", "title": "Anomie and forced division of labor"}], "description": "The point is to compare cohesion in traditional and modern societies."}, {"id": "5437299e-741f-4ccd-b47e-f2fa1fce5ab3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Suicide, Religion, and Ritual", "children": [{"id": "407d5b50-6dc6-45ab-95e3-fc70b82d94bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Egoistic, altruistic, anomic, and fatalistic suicide"}, {"id": "f138e63d-e377-4902-8435-0d4f7c2bd2c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Integration and regulation"}, {"id": "0f1ea616-55eb-437b-a9c7-230f54537c2f", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacred and profane"}, {"id": "cf5bb3ba-fd54-4032-96d2-862d8f1fc584", "type": "concept", "title": "Ritual, classification, and collective effervescence"}], "description": "Students learn how Durkheim connects individual action to social integration and collective meaning."}]}, {"id": "56abe1ed-beed-4529-8ace-13bedc556e84", "type": "topic", "title": "Max Weber: Interpretive Sociology, Rationalization, Authority, and Bureaucracy", "description": "Weber teaches students to analyze meaning, power, legitimacy, and rationalization. His work helps explain modern bureaucracy, authority, capitalism, religion, and the tension between values and facts.", "children": [{"id": "d5990417-2e12-4651-bc9a-622776c0e16d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Interpretive Sociology", "children": [{"id": "3fc6c34f-ae64-4066-b2bd-f86488b92809", "type": "concept", "title": "Verstehen"}, {"id": "4fef0ebe-b56c-41d8-85be-5d9bc08a1d42", "type": "concept", "title": "Types of social action"}, {"id": "f8ab74dc-9e3b-4c1a-83b6-fc63cbff2424", "type": "concept", "title": "Ideal types"}, {"id": "61e3cbcd-1756-45fe-976d-6b511dbda9fd", "type": "concept", "title": "Value relevance and objectivity"}], "description": "Students learn Weber's method for understanding meaningful social action without reducing it to psychology."}, {"id": "e51faaf0-4a93-436f-bbad-93f2e8b8ef2b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Rationalization and Disenchantment", "children": [{"id": "df4ca1ba-731d-4bf0-9c91-1ffa88941ac7", "type": "concept", "title": "Formal and substantive rationality"}, {"id": "6176b410-0b7b-4f51-a9e1-d22e56c96b03", "type": "concept", "title": "The Protestant ethic thesis"}, {"id": "16884b17-cfcd-4b55-9d86-ec83862074b8", "type": "concept", "title": "Disenchantment"}, {"id": "75120ea2-0687-4483-9c26-e6e8cc4a095a", "type": "concept", "title": "The iron cage"}], "description": "The gist is learning Weber's account of modern life as increasingly calculable, rule-bound, and disenchanted."}, {"id": "41d80c5b-aebc-47d8-8eba-f6bb95fc34c9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Authority, Bureaucracy, and Domination", "children": [{"id": "85ece242-3981-4c1c-bc36-2dd655d10396", "type": "concept", "title": "Traditional, charismatic, and legal-rational authority"}, {"id": "52a2b785-1ffc-41bc-8b4c-775b887f7b94", "type": "concept", "title": "Bureaucratic organization"}, {"id": "e74dc3bb-34f9-4ac1-a517-8e83b4b541e2", "type": "concept", "title": "Status groups and parties"}, {"id": "71ab5bcb-6a5b-4e01-894e-f19da18f8f36", "type": "concept", "title": "Politics, ethics, and responsibility"}], "description": "Students learn how power becomes legitimate and how modern administration shapes social life."}]}, {"id": "14beafe7-6380-4b69-9708-46377d4e4b58", "type": "topic", "title": "Georg Simmel: Forms of Association, Money, Metropolis, and Strangerhood", "description": "Simmel helps students see social life in forms of interaction: exchange, conflict, secrecy, sociability, distance, and individuality. His work bridges micro encounters and macro modernity.", "children": [{"id": "28c9b4e4-0e0a-400f-b651-ea0fae72566a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Forms of Association", "children": [{"id": "f82bc059-3a4a-4217-a2d6-3449853145e8", "type": "concept", "title": "Dyads and triads"}, {"id": "097d0332-54e4-498c-8bce-3be94dfa30ee", "type": "concept", "title": "Exchange and reciprocity"}, {"id": "d5951c1a-8bba-418b-a97b-ff3a86460189", "type": "concept", "title": "Conflict as social form"}, {"id": "e8db694f-a0b1-47f0-a321-83852b102f5a", "type": "concept", "title": "Sociability, secrecy, and trust"}], "description": "Students learn to analyze recurring interaction patterns across different social contents."}, {"id": "5c8e2c69-9301-4ff3-ae0b-a4910adc9293", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Money and Modern Life", "children": [{"id": "201b81f6-1d8b-4040-aecb-1a53cb21b345", "type": "concept", "title": "The philosophy of money"}, {"id": "74eb78e1-aaef-4ec1-ada2-aeec32275184", "type": "concept", "title": "Objective and subjective culture"}, {"id": "2a4708d5-508e-4e57-8869-f322bca46d5d", "type": "concept", "title": "Calculation and abstraction"}, {"id": "2cbb5cc4-bbef-41e9-bf2c-3ce0ffd228a1", "type": "concept", "title": "Freedom, dependence, and impersonality"}], "description": "The point is to understand how money transforms value, individuality, relationships, and social distance."}, {"id": "be4eb59c-5f30-4e14-9a33-096dd5d57f2b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The Metropolis and the Stranger", "children": [{"id": "001715ae-7d11-477e-a8f5-a9f1b6f460a5", "type": "concept", "title": "Blase attitude"}, {"id": "a1e47a78-6d60-47a5-913a-c3c051466de9", "type": "concept", "title": "Nervous stimulation"}, {"id": "6c120b82-0da2-4341-a450-3e1d40ad51eb", "type": "concept", "title": "The stranger as near and far"}, {"id": "c582f6d1-6e03-4166-8b25-d47fb46843b4", "type": "concept", "title": "Individuality and modern culture"}], "description": "Students learn how urban life produces distinctive forms of perception, identity, and social distance."}]}, {"id": "577f8c42-a1fa-48ae-8d06-546e1faefb58", "type": "topic", "title": "W.E.B. Du Bois: The Color Line, Double Consciousness, and Racialized Modernity", "description": "Du Bois belongs at the center of classical theory. Students learn how race, empire, capitalism, knowledge, and democracy are inseparable in his account of modern society.", "children": [{"id": "5453d26d-8fc7-4294-ab89-9c88795dc47f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The Color Line and Racialized Modernity", "children": [{"id": "92c9f37e-4e6d-4229-b2c1-b785b209a620", "type": "concept", "title": "The problem of the color line"}, {"id": "22e3278e-d585-43c0-9bea-9c86229dae8a", "type": "concept", "title": "Race, labor, and capitalism"}, {"id": "f856d725-5b6c-451f-8a36-c295ffeae2c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Empire and colonial domination"}, {"id": "985aa7bf-1f47-41e9-823f-2a305c06bf63", "type": "concept", "title": "Reconstruction and democracy"}], "description": "Students learn Du Bois's argument that the modern world is structured by global racial hierarchy."}, {"id": "401e6d8e-e588-414a-ba12-6f51f193dc19", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Double Consciousness and the Veil", "children": [{"id": "41083178-06c5-4055-871e-309461931b3b", "type": "concept", "title": "The veil"}, {"id": "e4508bc8-3341-4c10-84ba-40253a65f8b9", "type": "concept", "title": "Two-ness and self-consciousness"}, {"id": "4f895e1a-3d56-47e1-a2c9-ef234e4e7e1a", "type": "concept", "title": "Recognition and misrecognition"}, {"id": "40f2e118-9e69-44f1-9bd3-e5701b11e89f", "type": "concept", "title": "Identity, domination, and agency"}], "description": "The gist is understanding subjectivity under racial domination as a social and historical condition."}, {"id": "58dcbb52-a9ee-46f0-9db1-8e8cac19832a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Empirical Sociology and Public Scholarship", "children": [{"id": "5d9333a7-aee7-4087-885d-40b4d71b558f", "type": "concept", "title": "The Philadelphia Negro"}, {"id": "8ad01074-0fb3-4585-a405-f493c0db5d99", "type": "concept", "title": "Atlanta School sociology"}, {"id": "ada6f659-514e-4be6-874b-f649a6dcf6f3", "type": "concept", "title": "Data visualization and community study"}, {"id": "7dc342db-4b13-47ca-b304-e5af1e8ca496", "type": "concept", "title": "Scholarship, activism, and public sociology"}], "description": "Students learn Du Bois as a rigorous empirical researcher and public intellectual."}]}, {"id": "2f12cf7d-bc32-4e9f-b89b-8b3d0365f127", "type": "topic", "title": "Harriet Martineau and Charlotte Perkins Gilman: Gender, Political Economy, and Reform", "description": "This unit widens the classical canon. Martineau and Gilman show that early sociology included rigorous method, translation, gender analysis, political economy, and reformist critique.", "children": [{"id": "f2916a8e-da64-48c6-8aad-f52c50ab00c9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Harriet Martineau and Observational Method", "children": [{"id": "8888818d-6abd-422e-ab41-91c567147a42", "type": "concept", "title": "Society in America"}, {"id": "6ea74586-4d18-4d25-8ec7-e70f4263bb87", "type": "concept", "title": "Morals and manners"}, {"id": "f0f2ead4-f15f-4102-9b48-662b2407ffcc", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparative observation"}, {"id": "d706d630-ccea-4fd6-8ff2-02e618b6c916", "type": "concept", "title": "Abolition, democracy, and reform"}], "description": "Students learn Martineau's contributions to comparative observation, morals and manners, democracy, and social reform."}, {"id": "38c6f129-cafe-4ad3-bdd8-189b60d99624", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Charlotte Perkins Gilman and Gendered Political Economy", "children": [{"id": "6c7cbb81-5acb-4b57-864d-c15e0c3e7545", "type": "concept", "title": "Women and Economics"}, {"id": "e142d002-e247-4b9c-921d-9d98c3f1e53c", "type": "concept", "title": "Domestic labor and dependency"}, {"id": "47740a80-c987-4e17-91d2-9c8a14207057", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender socialization"}, {"id": "eab1a533-640f-41a6-be80-eab7919ddb6a", "type": "concept", "title": "Reform, utopia, and institutional change"}], "description": "The point is to analyze family, labor, dependency, and gender as social institutions rather than private arrangements."}, {"id": "abec580b-7d38-4e44-8443-bd1b98c0ac68", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Feminist Recovery of Classical Theory", "children": [{"id": "d6c490c3-c10c-4599-ae08-7e3c03c0b2ed", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon critique"}, {"id": "a3dd6ff3-d422-41d5-baf7-9cef7b48f68a", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender and knowledge production"}, {"id": "9b617f87-5baf-45ce-b958-0b9fcbfef2fe", "type": "concept", "title": "Public writing and reform"}, {"id": "536dc248-17c6-4f61-90aa-5829bb33eb72", "type": "concept", "title": "Intersection with race, class, and empire"}], "description": "Students learn how recovering excluded thinkers changes the questions theory asks."}]}, {"id": "7b025a23-8976-4f03-9e57-27e3232789d0", "type": "topic", "title": "Religion, Morality, Secularization, and Collective Life", "description": "Religion is central to classical theory because it reveals how societies create meaning, authority, discipline, morality, solidarity, and conflict.", "children": [{"id": "8deec7af-7bfd-4019-9098-bf3fbf7d467d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Durkheim on Ritual and Collective Life", "children": [{"id": "628a9995-3b48-4281-b4c7-6dcc80b91e26", "type": "concept", "title": "Sacred and profane"}, {"id": "9c399834-ca41-4d09-acd5-fee9ea1ff781", "type": "concept", "title": "Totemism and classification"}, {"id": "74f5615c-9c8f-463e-a11d-05368b93413b", "type": "concept", "title": "Collective effervescence"}, {"id": "704207c9-ec78-4cbe-801a-fa668f87ee63", "type": "concept", "title": "Ritual and solidarity"}], "description": "Students learn religion as a social fact that produces moral community."}, {"id": "32df2fbc-e645-4cfb-80d2-a95b03716908", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Weber on Religion and Capitalism", "children": [{"id": "27c1b573-93d5-412d-a5aa-08e49e011637", "type": "concept", "title": "Ascetic Protestantism"}, {"id": "d50370c4-0fb1-4e5e-8bf9-a39ca1582eb6", "type": "concept", "title": "Calling and discipline"}, {"id": "53eef9a3-e190-4293-9d99-dcf4d5d78c16", "type": "concept", "title": "World religions and comparative sociology"}, {"id": "dc6ba601-5754-43da-aa0e-dc0e5e71bfbe", "type": "concept", "title": "Disenchantment and secularization"}], "description": "The gist is understanding religion as an active force in economic and social organization."}, {"id": "883577f6-68c7-49cf-bb33-1a6652112e2b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Marx and Critical Accounts of Religion", "children": [{"id": "561d62c2-4559-4120-9045-1b0add91b1f1", "type": "concept", "title": "Religion and alienation"}, {"id": "cbcaf08b-ebc9-48eb-80bd-c3938254f65c", "type": "concept", "title": "Ideology and material conditions"}, {"id": "e91d0454-eca9-46fb-8fcf-59dbdb2e143f", "type": "concept", "title": "Legitimation and critique"}, {"id": "bff36f1b-9a04-43c3-83cd-ba78d86014eb", "type": "concept", "title": "Secularization debates"}], "description": "Students learn religion as consolation, ideology, protest, and social expression."}]}, {"id": "7d02ffee-8cc3-4e36-b46b-1dd70bd66711", "type": "topic", "title": "Urbanization, Immigration, the Chicago School, and Early Empirical Sociology", "description": "Early empirical sociology turned theory toward cities, migration, neighborhoods, race relations, and everyday social worlds. Students connect classical concepts to field research.", "children": [{"id": "af254c93-dad2-4806-baa7-b2101086b815", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Urbanization and Social Disorganization", "children": [{"id": "a5d366fb-d734-4572-9477-f713b604d024", "type": "concept", "title": "Industrial city and migration"}, {"id": "e54bf39b-02bf-4161-bf61-b05dca34e239", "type": "concept", "title": "Neighborhood ecology"}, {"id": "85b5d770-3ebe-4d66-84a9-1275d447a7d0", "type": "concept", "title": "Social disorganization"}, {"id": "ce74236c-e1ba-4e89-a4a4-74cde32742b6", "type": "concept", "title": "Community, poverty, and institutions"}], "description": "Students learn how rapid urban growth produced new questions about community, deviance, inequality, and social order."}, {"id": "af238291-6474-43f5-91a8-4ff905cb4a1b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The Chicago School", "children": [{"id": "153c8de1-26ce-4aa3-a595-127172fcc5bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Robert Park and Ernest Burgess"}, {"id": "fa5e7e85-aff6-4e69-a79b-bc9373c8a171", "type": "concept", "title": "Urban ecology"}, {"id": "5f49853e-3409-40f6-8743-6ddadd149b29", "type": "concept", "title": "Immigration and assimilation"}, {"id": "b07f6e67-84ed-4fa8-8496-5d874bc14156", "type": "concept", "title": "Case studies and ethnography"}], "description": "The point is to see how fieldwork, mapping, and urban observation became central to sociology."}, {"id": "328c29a8-eb43-4256-a25c-c34466aa2a4e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Race, Migration, and Urban Modernity", "children": [{"id": "d85786bc-63ae-4e6c-b651-c3d1d0a3fe63", "type": "concept", "title": "The ghetto and segregation"}, {"id": "16742ad3-82cb-486c-99ae-8df8516dcdbe", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethnic succession"}, {"id": "2ae51ba3-d6f6-454b-a1fd-8dc65eddda98", "type": "concept", "title": "Policing and deviance"}, {"id": "4034a98a-6c2c-4c51-8b59-89111ba23a04", "type": "concept", "title": "Du Bois and urban empirical sociology"}], "description": "Students learn to connect urban sociology to race, labor, segregation, and political power."}]}, {"id": "3890cb1b-5673-440d-bdb4-e0c437dbc46e", "type": "topic", "title": "Method, Evidence, Ideal Types, and Comparative-Historical Analysis", "description": "Theory is also method. Students learn how classical theorists constructed concepts, selected evidence, compared cases, and made arguments about history and society.", "children": [{"id": "880e56b2-65bc-4365-806e-d2b368f059e8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Close Reading and Conceptual Reconstruction", "children": [{"id": "b503d080-a707-4eb2-9e97-e25327040250", "type": "concept", "title": "Thesis, concept, and evidence"}, {"id": "b834efb6-7b0e-45a7-90c3-ae7b0b1d6c3e", "type": "concept", "title": "Contextual reading"}, {"id": "6831bb8f-edd6-41ba-8894-97a22a833660", "type": "concept", "title": "Key terms and definitions"}, {"id": "3daf7f26-eee6-4c6a-bda7-37883be201f5", "type": "concept", "title": "Interpretive charity and critique"}], "description": "Students learn to read difficult texts by identifying concepts, assumptions, claims, and tensions."}, {"id": "83874517-813a-4ae2-b012-97c54e566498", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ideal Types and Comparative-Historical Method", "children": [{"id": "488e1090-14f2-49d2-be84-04e4d679ec48", "type": "concept", "title": "Weberian ideal types"}, {"id": "a3d949ca-e878-4d04-9526-4d4edd84ecd1", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparative cases"}, {"id": "f2d3a02d-c7a5-480f-bacf-4ce7b0713f12", "type": "concept", "title": "Historical sequences"}, {"id": "eb922531-1d5b-460d-944b-340acde68db4", "type": "concept", "title": "Causal adequacy and interpretive adequacy"}], "description": "The gist is learning how theory turns messy reality into analyzable models without confusing models for reality."}, {"id": "4289c7ca-6d5f-403a-8560-70dd61688d18", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Evidence, Argument, and Writing Theory", "children": [{"id": "cd84b733-ebcf-4ab4-8382-1a8d252f7764", "type": "concept", "title": "Concept maps"}, {"id": "bee25c86-b334-4c03-ab37-534bf5724113", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparative matrices"}, {"id": "7cf383ab-f405-442a-8595-eae80e42f46a", "type": "concept", "title": "Textual evidence"}, {"id": "30b145ba-8ac0-4d9c-8247-0c72ff65b32a", "type": "concept", "title": "Application to empirical cases"}], "description": "Students learn to write theory papers that explain and evaluate concepts rather than merely summarize authors."}]}, {"id": "db49d705-4141-4267-86ce-45d71ea13c7f", "type": "topic", "title": "Power and Inequality: Class, Status, Race, Gender, Empire, and Coloniality", "description": "Classical theory is indispensable for studying inequality, but it must be read critically. Students learn how class, status, race, gender, empire, and coloniality shape modern power.", "children": [{"id": "4b3ce1b4-b43f-4334-afcc-247fe4ec217d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Class, Status, and Power", "children": [{"id": "0d513a43-9954-4eac-a34b-dc89b076b1c9", "type": "concept", "title": "Class relations"}, {"id": "e00115ac-26dc-4578-9062-c31a02dc07c4", "type": "concept", "title": "Status honor"}, {"id": "5219cac8-c5d6-4e92-9606-7aa690c627ae", "type": "concept", "title": "Party and power"}, {"id": "1bf9ea00-cd8e-40af-be04-aa3828a471e4", "type": "concept", "title": "Elites and domination"}], "description": "Students compare Marx's class analysis with Weber's multidimensional account of stratification."}, {"id": "254d70aa-0000-44fc-b12d-357852009f11", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Race, Gender, and Social Reproduction", "children": [{"id": "5f5ef73e-9d90-40be-bfb7-fe24f6fb3029", "type": "concept", "title": "Du Bois and racial capitalism"}, {"id": "6ee5034c-f1cd-4006-8658-9f8cc42b42b4", "type": "concept", "title": "Gilman and gendered labor"}, {"id": "e1e72249-f906-4a72-9d76-38220880ae5e", "type": "concept", "title": "Martineau and moral critique"}, {"id": "19d2b390-8ce6-46a3-ae5f-cd2d931ada6e", "type": "concept", "title": "Intersectional extensions"}], "description": "The point is to examine exclusions and recover theorists who analyzed domination beyond class alone."}, {"id": "2232de97-021a-48d7-85a0-00842fa3b0b9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Empire and Coloniality", "children": [{"id": "2ee2f605-e2f3-4426-bb75-563d28743ef5", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial labor and extraction"}, {"id": "68482d75-d5a3-4f5f-b93d-0679b357f8e2", "type": "concept", "title": "Race science and social hierarchy"}, {"id": "8581aabe-1008-4afc-b76f-3f75ab19f974", "type": "concept", "title": "Global color line"}, {"id": "87d6a758-506f-4425-b670-4904ab92b33f", "type": "concept", "title": "Decolonial critique of the canon"}], "description": "Students learn to ask how European modernity, capitalism, and theory were entangled with empire."}]}, {"id": "55a58ce3-ef79-4942-8895-0a4d3a61f1bd", "type": "topic", "title": "Comparing Classical Perspectives: Structure, Agency, Conflict, Order, and Meaning", "description": "This comparative unit helps students synthesize the course. They learn to use theory as a set of contrasting assumptions about human action, institutions, power, and social change.", "children": [{"id": "470a3222-a6f9-46b6-abc4-9cea51ce0374", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Structure and Agency", "children": [{"id": "2b1f1a89-192b-440d-9a5d-0371e1e4d048", "type": "concept", "title": "Marx and structural contradiction"}, {"id": "4247b937-5019-4794-a4a2-9373b1683f52", "type": "concept", "title": "Durkheim and social facts"}, {"id": "eefe0a74-488a-4220-b2bf-10194ca3b05b", "type": "concept", "title": "Weber and meaningful action"}, {"id": "5c74e1ff-3f8e-42d8-9bec-01f62848e74e", "type": "concept", "title": "Simmel and interaction forms"}], "description": "Students compare how theorists explain the relation between individual action and social constraint."}, {"id": "b27efa61-e748-4a36-8ecf-207806d43f8b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Conflict and Order", "children": [{"id": "9f9d5040-d3c2-4507-936e-0ab605d981be", "type": "concept", "title": "Class conflict"}, {"id": "bbe91f14-ed92-4107-a17b-1155a800f21c", "type": "concept", "title": "Moral integration"}, {"id": "be50cba5-8fcd-4fe6-bf51-cd84cd4fc679", "type": "concept", "title": "Legitimate domination"}, {"id": "1bde20cd-2b6e-44ce-95ef-e75afd4d4fb5", "type": "concept", "title": "Interactional tension and exchange"}], "description": "The gist is to compare theories that emphasize domination, cohesion, meaning, or institutional coordination."}, {"id": "f53f9dcf-4ee9-4e94-ad9f-0f31860ee76f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Meaning and Material Conditions", "children": [{"id": "e76718e5-2981-4e9d-8af8-ae8e3980bd08", "type": "concept", "title": "Ideology and material life"}, {"id": "c0425c38-e7e8-4ca4-b2ae-fb794b3836c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Religion and economic action"}, {"id": "0907f6ed-c84d-4c06-ad11-119561c1245b", "type": "concept", "title": "Collective representations"}, {"id": "3086d13c-bb33-4e6e-9653-b0e1e7ff5503", "type": "concept", "title": "Culture, money, race, and identity"}], "description": "Students learn to avoid false choices between culture and economy."}]}, {"id": "8eb51db3-8a2d-424a-84f8-27bc5294e819", "type": "topic", "title": "Classical Theory Today: Contemporary Relevance, Canon Critique, and Public Sociology", "description": "The final unit asks students to use classical theory now. Students apply concepts to contemporary problems while assessing what classical theory illuminates, distorts, or leaves out.", "children": [{"id": "e8c9f2d6-d7c5-4f4d-9135-2eabbf5aa17f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Applying Classical Theory to Contemporary Problems", "children": [{"id": "ea80ccbe-00c2-43dd-a3a6-794974f02734", "type": "concept", "title": "Platform capitalism and alienation"}, {"id": "40ec5605-d88d-4524-9e88-22713ba3aad7", "type": "concept", "title": "Bureaucracy, algorithms, and rationalization"}, {"id": "ea783c04-9eee-45f7-9eef-eb588dccc632", "type": "concept", "title": "Anomie, polarization, and social isolation"}, {"id": "77521bbb-d63b-457e-a403-deafb6451e27", "type": "concept", "title": "Race, inequality, and democracy"}], "description": "Students learn to use classical concepts in current analysis without forcing old frameworks onto new realities."}, {"id": "83ff1d00-fb69-4b8b-b7e3-8c6e0344757e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Critiquing the Canon", "children": [{"id": "ab2b9fd0-ee2c-4ced-bcf8-634ae9e41b22", "type": "concept", "title": "Eurocentrism and colonial blind spots"}, {"id": "c9f7b9a0-83e4-4360-84e0-ef41a64c9f45", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender exclusions"}, {"id": "5928ad04-424e-4bde-9a8d-dacbc693c31c", "type": "concept", "title": "Race and the marginalization of Du Bois"}, {"id": "d96e2e2f-5102-4df0-9b55-767f59f60633", "type": "concept", "title": "Translation, access, and disciplinary power"}], "description": "The point is to identify both enduring insight and historical limitation."}, {"id": "70a079b8-78a4-43fe-96e6-cb495f2642a9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Public Sociology and Theory as Practice", "children": [{"id": "604044eb-efdb-46cc-904f-7d0f1780b00d", "type": "concept", "title": "Theory-informed public writing"}, {"id": "003a6ca8-ddd2-462c-b092-70606003a225", "type": "concept", "title": "Concepts in policy debate"}, {"id": "c76d55d8-161a-40e5-8123-abbbb9b40ff7", "type": "concept", "title": "Critical reading of social claims"}, {"id": "7d286479-93e4-45b0-9f7a-265796e5d6ee", "type": "concept", "title": "Final synthesis and reflective practice"}], "description": "Students learn that theory can inform research, policy, activism, journalism, and civic reasoning."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$clstjson$::jsonb,
